Services.Guide.GoogleVoices=Reads books aloud in Google's own voices, over the internet. They are the best of the cloud voices for most languages, and the free allowance is the largest — around a million characters a month, which is two books.\n\nGoogle does not give these out as a key you can paste. It gives a SERVICE ACCOUNT: a small file ending in .json that you download once.\n\n1. Open console.cloud.google.com in your browser and sign in with a Google account.\n2. Accept the terms if you are asked, and create a project when it offers to. Any name will do.\n3. Find the search box at the top, type "Cloud Text-to-Speech API", choose it from the results and press Enable.\n4. Google will ask you to set up billing before it enables the API. A card is required even though the free allowance costs nothing.\n5. Search again for "Service accounts" and open it. Press Create service account, give it any name, and press Done. No role or permission needs choosing.\n6. Open the account you just made, go to the Keys tab, press Add key, then Create new key, and choose JSON. The file downloads.\n7. Come back here, press "Set up this service", and choose that downloaded file.\n\nNemoviz keeps what is inside the file, not the file itself, so you can move or delete the download afterwards.
Services.Guide.AzureVoices=Reads books aloud in Microsoft's Azure voices. Very good in English and many other languages; for Croatian they are ordinary, and Azure does not support the pronunciation dictionary there. The free allowance is about half a million characters a month, roughly one book.\n\nNemoviz makes the Azure resource for you, so there is no portal to work through. What you have to do yourself is open the Azure account, because that needs identity checks and an agreement — a person has to do that, deliberately.\n\n1. Open azure.microsoft.com/free in your browser and press Start free.\n2. Sign in with a Microsoft account, or make one.\n3. Fill in your details. Azure asks for a card to prove you are a person; the free tier is not charged.\n4. When the subscription is made, Azure quietly creates a DIRECTORY for it. If your account is a personal one — Gmail, Outlook, Hotmail — you will need its name, which ends in onmicrosoft.com. It is shown in the address bar when you are signed in to the portal, after the @ sign.\n5. Come back here and press "Set up this service".\n6. Fill in that directory name if your account is a personal one, and leave it empty if it is a work or school account. Then press Start.\n7. Open the sign-in page with the button, type the short code shown, and sign in.\n8. Choose your subscription and press Create. Nemoviz makes the resource and keeps its key. You type nothing else.
Services.Guide.DeepSeek=Translates a whole book into another language. Nemoviz judged it the steadiest of the services it can use, and it is the cheapest of them by a wide margin.\n\nThis one is a key you paste, and it is the simplest of the four to set up.\n\n1. Open platform.deepseek.com in your browser.\n2. Press Sign up. You can fill in an e-mail and password, or choose to sign up with Google, which is quicker.\n3. Confirm your e-mail if you are asked to.\n4. Once you are signed in, find Top up or Billing and add a small amount of credit. Translation is charged by the amount of text and a book costs very little, but there is no free allowance.\n5. Find API keys in the menu on the left.\n6. Press Create new API key, give it any name, and press Create.\n7. THE KEY IS SHOWN ONCE. Copy it before you close that window.\n8. Come back here, press "Set up this service", and paste it in.
Services.Guide.Gemini=Translates a whole book into another language, using Google's Gemini. Judged the best of them by ear, and it has a free allowance — but it refuses roughly every sixth passage of an ordinary novel for reasons nothing we can set will prevent, so Nemoviz falls back to another service when it does.\n\nThis one is a key you paste.\n\n1. Open aistudio.google.com in your browser and sign in with a Google account.\n2. Accept the terms if you are asked.\n3. Find Get API key, at the top left or under your account.\n4. Press Create API key. If it asks which project, take the one it offers or make a new one — any name will do.\n5. Copy the key it shows you.\n6. Come back here, press "Set up this service", and paste it in.\n\nThe free allowance is enough to try it on a book. Paid use is set up in the same place, under Billing.
